  38 of them are new for r9: 20 for rule D1, 18 for rule N1.
  Split by harness: JSON->validator, document->validator, board record->board gate,
  dependency GRAPH->board gate (the graph harness is new in r9; D1's reachability half
  cannot be tested by corrupting the contract, which is why eight revisions of a
  1331-check validator never saw the defect).

  Three defects in the NEW gates were found by their own negative mutations and fixed
  rather than worked around:
    a) R37's "declared but explained nowhere" check was TAUTOLOGICAL. It compared the
       declared set against ids mentioned anywhere in the contract — and the consumers
       list is itself a string in the contract, so the question answered itself. A
       mutation that appended a consumer id appearing nowhere else PASSED. Fixed by
       reading mentions from everywhere ELSE (excluding `consumers` and rule D1's own
       register), in both the validator and the board gate.
    b) A graph mutation premised on cutting TASK-260715-3jloqy's edge orphaned NOTHING,
       because TASK-260728-q5kjta also blocks 3jloqy and carries its own edge here. The
       premise was wrong, not the gate. Replaced by two accurate mutations: cutting the
       authorization node's own edge (orphans 1), and cutting BOTH edges carrying the
       portal chain (orphans 12). This is also the argument for a transitive CLOSURE
       rather than an edge check — the DAG has redundant paths.
    c) A document mutation did not apply because the target sentence is hard-wrapped;
       the replacement now spans the real line break instead of a normalised form.

